<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 21 L1b, AC2. HOW FAR BEHIND THE INPUT THE MONITORED OUTPUT IS.

  Runs with SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_BACKEND=file:../test_position.wav and
  SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED=1 (set on the CTest entry). RUN_SERIAL: this is a
  wall-clock latency assertion, so background load moves the answer.

  MEASURED BY CROSS-CORRELATION, not by the position decoder. The decoder
  resolves 4096-frame blocks (design F11) and the whole budget here is twice
  that, so it could not tell a pass from a fail. The correlation is normalised,
  so the peak is a SIMILARITY and the threshold means the same thing whatever
  the monitored level is.

  THE BUDGET, and where every term comes from:

    input block          1024   FileAudioInput::kBlockFrames, matched to the
                                capture backend's own block on purpose
    ring depth           4096   twLiveMixRingDefaultDepth (4) x 1024
    3 output blocks      3072   the RT's own grid, plus the pump's lead
    ..........................
    maxFrames            8192   which is the verb's default

  Nothing here is a claim about REAL hardware: WASAPI shared adds ~100 ms of
  its own (design F6) and proposal 35 (ASIO) is the prerequisite for a number
  a performer would call low. What this bounds is the part 21 owns - the ring,
  the pump's lead and the hand-off to the RT.

  MEASURED on the development box, 40 consecutive runs: 5120 frames = 106.7 ms
  in 33 of them and 6144 = 128 ms in the other 7, correlation 1.000 throughout.
  Five 1024-frame blocks is the resting value - one the input capture thread
  holds, the pump lead of two, two on the RT side - and the sixth appears when
  the file input jitters.

  WHY THE WINDOW IS 8192 FRAMES AND NOT 24000, which is the thing this case
  cost the most time to get right. FileAudioInput has its OWN clock
  (MidiOutScheduler::hostNowNs) while the capture backend paces the RT on its
  own, so the two drift; when a block is late the pump pulls short, zero-fills,
  and the frames it did not get are DELAYED rather than lost - so the lag steps
  up by one input block and stays there. A 24000-frame window can therefore
  span a lag CHANGE, and no single lag correlates well with a window that has
  two of them in it: measured 0.88 and 0.69 in 2 of 40 runs, which is a
  correctly-reported change of alignment and not a monitoring fault. A window
  short enough to sit inside one alignment (8192 = ~170 ms) measures the lag
  the case is about, and the correlation floor stays at 0.9 rather than being
  lowered to swallow the artefact. A REAL device does not have this failure
  mode at all: input and output share one clock.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="monitor_latency">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <!-- No insert: a plugin would change the level but not the alignment, and
         the alignment is the whole assertion. Channel mask 1 = device channel
         0; test_position.wav is mono. -->
    <set-track-input trackPath="0" input="audio:file:1"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>

    <!-- Transport STOPPED: the live lane is the ONLY producer, so nothing in
         the recording can be mistaken for the arrangement. -->
    <wait-ms ms="2500"/>
    <dump-playback-capture filename="lat.wav" minFrames="96000"/>

    <assert-monitor-latency filename="lat.wav" inputFile="../test_position.wav"
                            channel="0" startFrame="60000" frameCount="8192"
                            searchFrames="24000" minCorrelation="0.9"
                            maxFrames="8192"/>

    <!-- The recording really is the input, not silence the correlator happened
         to like: the position fixture is a 0.5-amplitude tone, RMS 0.3536. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="lat.wav" startFrame="48000" frameCount="24000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.25" maxRms="0.45"/>

    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="0"/>
    <assert-render-policy liveThreadRefusals="0" liveOwnedRefusals="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
